  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "Letters named after the body",
      "body": "<p>Each of the eighteen original letters, the Iyek Ipee, takes its name from a part of the human body: <span lang=\"mni\">ꯀ</span> kok is the head, <span lang=\"mni\">ꯁ</span> sam the hair, <span lang=\"mni\">ꯃ</span> mit the eye and <span lang=\"mni\">ꯅ</span> na the ear. Meitei tradition holds that the letters were modelled on the body itself, and teachers still use the names as a mnemonic.</p>\n<p>The nine Lom Eeyek letters were added later for sounds that came in with borrowed words.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "A script brought back",
      "body": "<p>Meetei Mayek was the script of the kingdom of Manipur for centuries. In the early 18th century the Bengali script replaced it, and for about two hundred years Manipuri was written in Bengali letters.</p>\n<p>A revival movement in the 20th century restored the old script, and the government of Manipur adopted it in 1980. Since the 2000s it has been taught in schools and used on signboards beside the Bengali script, which is still common in print.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Lonsum and the syllable",
      "body": "<p>Manipuri syllables often end in a consonant. Instead of a vowel-killing sign, Meetei Mayek has eight final letters, the lonsum, each a short form of an ordinary letter: <span lang=\"mni\">ꯀ</span> becomes <span lang=\"mni\">ꯛ</span> at the end of <span lang=\"mni\">ꯀꯣꯛ</span> (kok).</p>\n<p>For other clusters there is a sign, the apun iyek, that joins two letters with no vowel between them, as in words borrowed from Sanskrit.</p>"
    }
  ]
